 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 21,
   "y": 12,
   "plaque": "kilkenny_statutes",
   "title": "The Statutes of Kilkenny",
   "text": "The Normans landed in Ireland in 1169, and within two centuries many had gone native, speaking Irish, wearing Irish dress and marrying into Irish families: more Irish than the Irish themselves, as the saying went. In 1366 a parliament here passed the Statutes of Kilkenny to stop it, banning settlers from Irish speech, law and marriage. It did not work.",
   "people": "normans"
  },
  {
   "text": "THE MEDIEVAL MILE. A kilometre of cobbles between the castle at one end and St Canice's Cathedral at the other. In the Middle Ages it was one of the busiest streets in Ireland. Now it sells sweaters.",
   "x": 9.0,
   "y": 22.0
  },
  {
   "text": "KILKENNY CASTLE. Built by the Normans in 1195, home to the Butler family for nearly 600 years, and rebuilt in the 1800s with some grand ideas. The long gallery is lined with paintings of Butlers looking smug. The park has none.",
   "x": 16.0,
   "y": 12.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE ROSE GARDEN. Kilkenny's castle park is a lovely place to sit. The Butlers had gardeners for it. You have a bench.",
   "x": 36.0,
   "y": 12.0
  },
  {
   "text": "ST CANICE'S. A 13th-century cathedral with a 9th-century round tower beside it. If you climb the tower's 121 steps you can see five counties. If you stay up there you can see six.",
   "x": 48.0,
   "y": 20.0
  },
  {
   "text": "ROTHE HOUSE. A Tudor merchant's home from 1594, with three courtyards, a hidden garden and very proud beams.",
   "x": 40.0,
   "y": 20.0
  },
  {
   "text": "KYTELER'S INN. Named for Dame Alice Kyteler, who was accused of witchcraft in 1324. She left town in a hurry. Her maid wasn't so lucky. The pub is now much nicer than the history.",
   "x": 45.0,
   "y": 20.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE BUTTER SLIP. A narrow medieval passage down from the High Street, where butter was sold, and slipped about. Please don't slip.",
   "x": 17.0,
   "y": 29.0
  },
  {
   "x": 13,
   "y": 28,
   "text": "BLUE PLAQUE: The Confederation of Kilkenny, the Irish Catholic government of the 1640s, first met in this city in 1642.",
   "kind": "plaque",
   "dx": -0.475,
   "plaque": "kilkenny_confederation",
   "title": "Confederate Kilkenny"
  },
  {
   "x": 41,
   "y": 18,
   "plaque": "kilkenny_smithwicks",
   "title": "Smithwick's Brewery",
   "text": "In 1710 John Smithwick set up a brewery in Kilkenny on the grounds of the old St Francis Abbey, where Franciscan friars had brewed ale long before. The red ale is said 'Smiddicks', and saying it any other way is a sure sign of a visitor."
  },
  {
   "x": 54,
   "y": 13,
   "plaque": "kilkenny_black_abbey",
   "title": "The Black Abbey",
   "text": "The Black Abbey is a Dominican priory founded in 1225 by William Marshal the younger. It takes its name from the black cloaks the friars wore over their white habits. It is still in use, and has a very fine stained-glass rosary window."
  },
  {
   "x": 8,
   "y": 20,
   "plaque": "kilkenny_college",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Kilkenny College",
   "text": "Kilkenny College counts two great Irish writers among its old pupils. Jonathan Swift went on to write Gulliver's Travels, and George Berkeley became a philosopher and bishop. The city of Berkeley in California is named after him, though they say it differently."
  },
  {
   "x": 19,
   "y": 30,
   "plaque": "kilkenny_cat_laughs",
   "title": "The Cat Laughs",
   "text": "Every summer since the 1990s Kilkenny has hosted the Cat Laughs, a comedy festival that fills pubs, halls and the odd church with comedians from all over the world. It is named after the Kilkenny cats, who were not known for their sense of humour."
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 24,
   "plaque": "landsky_kilkenny_record",
   "title": "Ireland's hottest day",
   "text": "On 26 June 1887 a thermometer at Kilkenny Castle read 33.3C, which is still the highest temperature ever recorded in Ireland. Weather has been measured here a long time: Valentia Observatory in Kerry has kept records since 1868, and John Tyndall of Leighlinbridge, up the road in County Carlow, proved in 1859 that water vapour and carbon dioxide trap the heat leaving the Earth, which is the whole of the greenhouse effect in one experiment."
  }
